---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/data';
---

<Layout
  title="How to read US goods trade"
  description="Understand general imports, exports, nominal values, HS classifications, missing data, changes and revisions."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">BEHIND THE NUMBERS</p>
    <h1>Know what a figure means.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A meaningful comparison starts with the same product definition, partner,
      flow, period and statistical basis.
    </p>
    <h2>The statistical basis</h2>
    <dl class="definition-list">
      <dt>US reporter, goods only</dt>
      <dd>
        Countries are US trading partners. Services and trade between two non-US
        countries are outside this dataset.
      </dd>
      <dt>Imports and exports</dt>
      <dd>
        General imports use customs value. Total exports use FAS value. Neither
        is a goods-and-services balance-of-payments series.
      </dd>
      <dt>Monthly, nominal USD</dt>
      <dd>
        Values are current dollars, not adjusted for inflation or seasonal
        effects. A dollar increase does not necessarily mean more physical
        goods.
      </dd>
      <dt>HS2022 chapters</dt>
      <dd>
        Two-digit chapters group products. Detailed import HTSUS codes and
        export Schedule B codes are not interchangeable. Classification changes
        require explicit concordance.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <h2>Changes and comparisons</h2>
    <p>
      Year-over-year change is (current / previous-year value − 1) × 100. Dollar
      changes use exact integer arithmetic. Displayed percentages are rounded
      directly from the exact ratio to one decimal place, with half values
      rounded away from zero; CSV values preserve integer dollars. A zero
      baseline has no defined percentage growth. Missing values remain
      unavailable.
    </p>
    <p>
      Market shares require a source-consistent world denominator. We never use
      the sum of displayed countries as if it represented the world. Country
      profiles in this preview sum only the four selected chapters.
    </p>
    <h2>Missing and suppressed information</h2>
    <p>
      Not available, suppressed, not reported and reported zero are separate
      states. A gap in a chart is not a zero. We do not reconstruct suppressed
      values. Quantities are omitted at HS2 because combining unlike units would
      be misleading.
    </p>
    <h2>Four different dates</h2>
    <p>
      The data period describes when trade occurred. The official release date
      describes publication; the official revision date describes a
      publisher-announced revision. Ingestion time describes our retrieval. An
      unknown official date remains unknown. Sample fixture generation is not an
      official release date.
    </p>
    <h2>What the numbers cannot tell you</h2>
    <p>
      Measured changes alone do not establish a cause, market price, tariff
      liability or business opportunity. The sample preview does not describe
      actual trade and should not support business decisions.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={href('sources/')}>Inspect the source definitions →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
